// Title: Suspicious Recursive Takeown
// Sigma ID: 554601fb-9b71-4bcc-abf4-21a611be4fde
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: frack113
// Date: 2022-01-30 | Modified: 2022-11-21
// ATT&CK: defense-impairment, T1222.001
// Description: Adversaries can interact with the DACLs using built-in Windows commands takeown which can grant
//   adversaries higher permissions on specific files and folders
// False positives:
//   - Scripts created by developers and admins
//   - Administrative activity
// References:
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/takeown
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/f339e7da7d05f6057fdfcdd3742bfcf365fee2a9/atomics/T1222.001/T1222.001.md#atomic-test-1---take-ownership-using-takeown-utility
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_takeown_recursive_own.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "\\takeown.exe" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "/f " and ProcessCommandLine contains "/r")
